<article class="env-lemma" id="09ME">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09ME">Lemma <span data-tag="09ME">10.120.16</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a ring. Let $I$ and $J$ be nonzero ideals of $A$ such that $IJ = (f)$ for some nonzerodivisor $f \in A$. Then $I$ and $J$ are finitely generated ideals and finitely locally free of rank $1$ as $A$-modules. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      It suffices to show that $I$ and $J$ are finite locally free $A$-modules of rank $1$, see Lemma <a href="/tag/00NX" data-tag="00NX">10.78.2</a>. To do this, write $f = \sum _{i = 1, \ldots , n} x_ i y_ i$ with $x_ i \in I$ and $y_ i \in J$. We can also write $x_ i y_ i = a_ i f$ for some $a_ i \in A$. Since $f$ is a nonzerodivisor we see that $\sum a_ i = 1$. Thus it suffices to show that each $I_{a_ i}$ and $J_{a_ i}$ is free of rank $1$ over $A_{a_ i}$. After replacing $A$ by $A_{a_ i}$ we conclude that $f = xy$ for some $x \in I$ and $y \in J$. Note that both $x$ and $y$ are nonzerodivisors. We claim that $I = (x)$ and $J = (y)$ which finishes the proof. Namely, if $x' \in I$, then $x'y = af = axy$ for some $a \in A$. Hence $x' = ax$ and we win. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>